from tp_common import *
C = "sat"; L = "SAT"; OUT = "../../courses/sat/sat-2.html"
TAGS = {"i": "Main idea", "e": "Evidence", "n": "Inference", "v": "Words in context", "s": "Structure & purpose", "x": "Cross-text"}
P = lambda *a, **k: psg(*a, **k)

MAIN = topic("main", "Central ideas and details", f'''
<p>"Which choice best states the main idea?" The right answer covers the <b>whole</b> text, not one sentence, and claims no more than the text supports.</p>
{P("In the 1950s, the biologist Rachel Carson began collecting reports of birds dying after farmers sprayed the pesticide DDT. Rather than relying on a single study, she gathered evidence from scientists, doctors and farmers across the country. Her 1962 book, <i>Silent Spring</i>, combined this evidence into an argument the public could follow, and it helped lead to new limits on pesticide use.")}
{worked("Main idea?", "Carson persuaded the public by gathering evidence from many sources into one clear argument, which helped change pesticide rules. <br><b>Traps:</b> \"Carson studied birds\" is a detail; \"DDT was banned worldwide in 1962\" claims more than the text says.")}
{box("tip", "Too narrow, too broad, or off", "Wrong main-idea choices are usually one detail (too narrow), a sweeping claim (too broad), or something the text never says (off). The right answer is boring and accurate.")}
''', pill=("i", "2.1"))

EVID = topic("evidence", "Command of evidence: text and data", f'''
<p>Two kinds: <b>textual</b> (which quotation or finding supports a claim) and <b>quantitative</b> (which choice correctly uses a table or graph). For both, first state the <b>exact claim</b>, then test each choice against it.</p>
{hl("Find the evidence", [
 {"claim": "Claim: The researcher believes ants choose routes based on distance, not on how crowded the path is.", "sents": ["The researcher placed two bridges between an ant nest and a food source.", "One bridge was twice as long as the other.", "Within an hour, almost all the ants used the shorter bridge.", "Even when the short bridge was packed with ants, newcomers still chose it.", "The researcher repeated the test with five colonies."], "a": 3, "x": "Ants kept choosing the short bridge even when it was crowded: distance wins over crowding.", "why": {"2": "Close, but this only shows they prefer the short bridge. It says nothing about crowding."}},
 {"claim": "Claim: The poem's speaker finds comfort in the city at night.", "sents": ["The streetlights hum their one low note,", "and trains go by like distant boats;", "I walk the empty blocks alone", "and feel the city keep me home."], "a": 3, "x": "\"Feel the city keep me home\" directly expresses comfort.", "why": {"2": "Walking alone could be lonely or calm; it doesn't show comfort on its own."}},
 {"claim": "Claim: The new library schedule increased weekend use.", "sents": ["The library opened on Sundays starting in March.", "Staff also added a reading room for teens.", "Weekend visits rose from 900 to 1,600 per month after March.", "Weekday visits stayed about the same."], "a": 2, "x": "That's the only sentence with data showing weekend use went up.", "why": {"0": "That's the change, not evidence of its effect."}}
])}
<p><b>Graphs and tables:</b> the right choice must be (1) <b>true</b> according to the data and (2) <b>relevant</b> to the claim in the passage. Many wrong choices are true but answer a different question.</p>
<div class="passage"><table class="dt"><tr><th>Seedling group</th><th>Light (hours/day)</th><th>Avg. height after 3 weeks (cm)</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>8</td><td>6.1</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>12</td><td>9.4</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>16</td><td>9.6</td></tr></table>
<p>A student claims that increasing light beyond 12 hours per day had little additional effect on growth.</p></div>
{worked("Which choice uses the data to support the claim?", "\"Group C, with 16 hours, averaged 9.6 cm, only slightly taller than group B's 9.4 cm.\" <br>\"Group B grew more than group A\" is true but is about going from 8 to 12 hours, not beyond 12.")}
''', pill=("e", "2.2"))

INF = topic("inference", "Inferences", f'''
<p>"Which choice most logically completes the text?" The blank is at the end. Follow the logic chain; the answer must <b>necessarily</b> follow, not just be possible.</p>
{P("Archaeologists found bread wheat seeds at a site dated to 7,000 years ago. Bread wheat does not grow wild in that region; it was first domesticated about 600 kilometers to the east. This suggests that <span class=blank></span>")}
{worked("Best completion?", "\"people at the site traded with, or came from, communities to the east.\" The seeds had to get there somehow, and wild growth is ruled out. <br>\"wheat was first domesticated at this site\" contradicts the text; \"bread was the main food\" goes beyond it.")}
''', pill=("n", "2.3"))

WIC = topic("wic", "Words in context", f'''
<p>Cover the choices. Read the sentence and put your own word in the blank. Then pick the choice closest to your word. Watch for <b>second meanings</b>: \"novel\" can mean new, \"qualify\" can mean limit, \"check\" can mean stop.</p>
{P("Although the scientist's early results were promising, she was careful to <span class=blank></span> her conclusions, noting that the sample was small and the study lasted only a month.")}
{worked("Your word first", "Something like \"limit\" or \"soften.\" Best choice: <b>qualify</b>. Traps: \"publish,\" \"celebrate,\" \"repeat\" don't match \"careful\" + small sample.")}
{table(["Word", "Common SAT meaning"], [["arresting", "striking, grabs attention"], ["temper", "to moderate or soften"], ["sanction", "approve OR penalize (context decides)"], ["prosaic", "ordinary, unimaginative"], ["equivocal", "unclear, open to more than one reading"], ["tenuous", "weak, thin"], ["corroborate", "confirm with evidence"], ["undermine", "weaken"], ["candid", "frank, honest"], ["pragmatic", "practical"], ["ambivalent", "having mixed feelings"], ["meticulous", "very careful about details"]])}
''', pill=("v", "2.4"))

STRUCT = topic("structure", "Structure, purpose and cross-text", f'''
<p><b>Purpose</b> questions ask why a sentence or text is there. Use verbs: introduces, illustrates, challenges, qualifies, explains a cause. <b>Structure</b> questions ask for the order of moves: \"describes a common view, then presents evidence against it.\"</p>
{P("For decades, scientists assumed that octopuses, being solitary animals, had little reason to recognize individual humans. <u>Yet in one aquarium, an octopus repeatedly sprayed water at a single staff member while ignoring everyone else.</u> Researchers now think octopuses may track individuals far more than expected.")}
{worked("Function of the underlined sentence?", "It gives an example that challenges the earlier assumption. \"Yet\" is your signal.")}
<p><b>Cross-text</b> questions give Text 1 and Text 2. Summarize each author's position in a few words, then ask: would Author 2 agree, disagree, or add a condition?</p>
{P("<b>Text 1:</b> Short daily practice sessions are the best way to learn an instrument, because the brain consolidates skills during sleep between sessions.", "<b>Text 2:</b> Daily practice helps beginners, but advanced musicians often need long, focused sessions to master difficult passages that can't be broken into small pieces.")}
{worked("How would the author of Text 2 respond to Text 1?", "Partly agree: short sessions work for beginners, but not for every learner. The answer will say Text 1's claim is <b>too general</b>.")}
''', pill=("s", "2.5"))

CH = [chapter("ch-ideas", "Information and Ideas", "Ideas", [MAIN, EVID, INF]), chapter("ch-craft", "Craft and Structure", "Craft", [WIC, STRUCT])]

PQ = [
 q(P("Many cities plant trees to cool streets in summer. A 2021 study of one city found that blocks with a dense tree canopy were up to 5°C cooler at midday than nearby blocks with few trees. The researchers also noted that the cooling effect was strongest on blocks where trees shaded the pavement directly.") + "Which choice best states the main idea of the text?", ["A study found that dense tree cover, especially trees shading pavement, cooled city blocks.", "Cities should plant trees on every block.", "Pavement is the main cause of summer heat.", "The 2021 study was the first to measure temperature."], 0, "Covers the whole text without overreaching.", "i"),
 q(P("The painter Jacob Lawrence often worked on a whole series at once. Instead of finishing one panel before starting the next, he applied a single color to every panel in the series, then moved to the next color. Lawrence said this helped the series look like one connected story.") + "According to the text, why did Lawrence paint one color at a time across all panels?", ["To make the series feel unified", "To save paint", "Because he had little time", "To make each panel look different"], 0, "\"Helped the series look like one connected story.\"", "i"),
 q(P("A team tested whether a new fertilizer helps tomato plants. Plants given the fertilizer produced an average of 31 tomatoes each; plants without it produced an average of 24.") + "Which choice most effectively uses the data to support the claim that the fertilizer increased yield?", ["Fertilized plants averaged 31 tomatoes, compared with 24 for unfertilized plants.", "Some plants produced more than 30 tomatoes.", "The team tested tomato plants.", "Unfertilized plants produced tomatoes."], 0, "Compares the two groups, which is what the claim needs.", "e"),
 q(P("Some birds that live in cities sing at a higher pitch than members of the same species in forests. Low-pitched city noise, like traffic, can drown out low notes. Higher songs may therefore <span class=blank></span>") + "Which choice most logically completes the text?", ["be easier for other birds to hear over city noise.", "attract more predators in forests.", "be learned from humans.", "make city birds larger."], 0, "Noise masks low notes, so high notes carry.", "n"),
 q(P("The novelist's early critics found her plots too <span class=blank></span>: each book's ending could be predicted from its first chapter.") + "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word?", ["formulaic", "intricate", "surprising", "poetic"], 0, "Predictable = formulaic.", "v"),
 q(P("Although the evidence for the theory was <span class=blank></span>, resting on a single fossil, many researchers accepted it immediately.") + "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word?", ["tenuous", "overwhelming", "familiar", "recent"], 0, "\"Although\" + single fossil → weak.", "v"),
 q(P("Bees are often described as hard-working and tireless. <u>Yet a study that tracked individual honeybees found that many spent most of the day resting inside the hive.</u> The researchers suggest that colonies may keep idle workers as a reserve for emergencies.") + "Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence?", ["It presents a finding that complicates a common description.", "It restates the common description.", "It explains how the study was funded.", "It offers a definition of a term."], 0, "\"Yet\" turns against the common view.", "s"),
 q(P("<b>Text 1:</b> Electric scooters reduce car traffic because riders use them for short trips they would otherwise drive.", "<b>Text 2:</b> Surveys show that most scooter trips replace walking or public transit, not car trips.") + "Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to Text 1?", ["By arguing that the premise about what scooters replace is mistaken", "By agreeing that scooters reduce car traffic", "By suggesting scooters are unsafe", "By claiming cars are cheaper"], 0, "Text 2 attacks the assumption that scooter trips replace car trips.", "x"),
 q(P("Explorer Ynés Mexía didn't begin collecting plants until she was 55. Over the next 13 years, she gathered about 145,000 specimens, including roughly 500 species new to science.") + "Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?", ["It notes a late start, then describes impressive results.", "It compares two explorers.", "It describes a method, then criticizes it.", "It poses a question and leaves it open."], 0, "Start at 55 → huge output.", "s"),
 q(P("A historian argues that the printing press changed how people stored knowledge. Before printing, scholars memorized long texts; after printing, they <span class=blank></span>") + "Which choice most logically completes the text?", ["could rely more on books instead of memory.", "stopped reading entirely.", "memorized even longer texts.", "wrote all books by hand."], 0, "Follows from \"changed how people stored knowledge.\"", "n"),
 q(P("The architect's design was <span class=blank></span>: rather than following any one tradition, it borrowed elements from Japanese, Scandinavian and Mexican building styles.") + "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word?", ["eclectic", "rigid", "unoriginal", "minimal"], 0, "Drawing from many sources = eclectic.", "v"),
 q(P("Bird species | Avg. wingspan (cm) | Migration distance (km)", "Arctic tern | 76 | 70,000", "Barn swallow | 33 | 10,000", "Ruby-throated hummingbird | 10 | 3,000") + "A student claims that among these birds, a larger wingspan is associated with a longer migration. Which choice best supports the claim?", ["The Arctic tern has the largest wingspan and the longest migration, and the hummingbird has the smallest of both.", "The barn swallow migrates 10,000 km.", "The hummingbird is the smallest bird.", "All three birds migrate."], 0, "Shows the pattern across the extremes.", "e"),
 q(P("Critics call the new film's dialogue <span class=blank></span>; characters speak in long, flowery speeches that no real person would give.") + "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word?", ["stilted", "natural", "terse", "witty"], 0, "Unnatural, stiff = stilted.", "v"),
 q(P("Many people think deserts are lifeless. But after rare heavy rains, seeds that have waited in the soil for years can sprout and bloom within days, covering the ground in color.") + "What is the main purpose of the text?", ["To correct a misconception by describing desert blooms", "To explain how to plant a garden", "To argue deserts are expanding", "To compare deserts with forests"], 0, "\"Many people think … But …\"", "s"),
]
CHAL = [
 q(P("<b>Text 1:</b> Linguist A argues that children learn grammar mainly by imitating adults.", "<b>Text 2:</b> Linguist B points out that young children often produce forms like \"goed\" and \"mouses,\" which adults almost never say. B concludes that children build rules on their own and overapply them.") + "Based on the texts, how would Linguist B most likely respond to Linguist A?", ["Imitation can't fully explain errors that children never hear adults make.", "Children never imitate adults.", "Adults should stop correcting children.", "Grammar can't be learned at all."], 0, "B's evidence is forms that can't come from imitation; but B doesn't say imitation plays no role.", "x"),
 q(P("Researchers studying an ancient trade route found glass beads made with a chemical recipe used only in one Egyptian workshop. The beads turned up in sites more than 3,000 km away. Because the recipe was never written down and was closely guarded, the researchers concluded that <span class=blank></span>") + "Which choice most logically completes the text?", ["the beads were likely made in that workshop and carried along trade routes.", "workshops far away independently invented the same recipe.", "the beads were made recently.", "the workshop was located 3,000 km from Egypt."], 0, "A secret, unrecorded recipe makes independent invention unlikely; transport explains the distance.", "n"),
 q(P("The essayist's tone toward technology is <span class=blank></span>: she praises the way phones connect distant families but worries about how they pull attention away from people in the same room.") + "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word?", ["ambivalent", "hostile", "reverent", "indifferent"], 0, "Mixed feelings.", "v"),
 q(P("A scientist hypothesized that a certain lizard's dark color helps it warm up faster in the morning. She measured how quickly dark and light lizards of the same species reached 30°C in the sun.") + "Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the hypothesis?", ["Dark and light lizards reached 30°C in about the same time.", "Dark lizards were more common in cold areas.", "Light lizards were slightly smaller.", "Both lizards ate the same insects."], 0, "If color doesn't change warming time, the hypothesis loses support.", "e"),
 q(P("Some historians have dismissed medieval maps as inaccurate. <u>But these maps were often not meant to guide travelers; many were designed to show religious and historical ideas, placing Jerusalem at the center to express its importance.</u>") + "Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion?", ["It argues the maps should be judged by their actual purpose.", "It agrees the maps were inaccurate and useless.", "It explains how medieval travelers navigated.", "It describes where Jerusalem is."], 0, "Reframes the criticism by explaining what the maps were for.", "s"),
 q(P("Year | Bicycle commuters in the city", "2015 | 4,200", "2018 | 6,900", "2021 | 6,700") + "A planner says that after new bike lanes opened in 2016, bicycle commuting rose sharply and then leveled off. Which choice best uses the data to support this?", ["Commuters rose from 4,200 in 2015 to 6,900 in 2018, then stayed near that level (6,700) in 2021.", "There were 6,700 commuters in 2021.", "Commuting fell between 2018 and 2021.", "Bike lanes opened in 2016."], 0, "Both parts of the claim: sharp rise, then leveling.", "e"),
]
EX = [
 q(P("In a study of 200 office workers, those who took a short walk after lunch reported feeling more alert in the afternoon than those who stayed at their desks.") + "Which choice best states the main finding?", ["A short walk after lunch was associated with greater afternoon alertness.", "Office workers dislike lunch.", "Walking cures fatigue for everyone.", "Desks cause tiredness."], 0, "Accurate and limited.", "i"),
 q(P("The poet's lines are <span class=blank></span>; in just a few words she captures an entire season.") + "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word?", ["concise", "rambling", "obscure", "lengthy"], 0, "Few words = concise.", "v"),
 q(P("Sea otters eat sea urchins, which eat kelp. Where otters disappeared, urchin populations exploded. Scientists therefore expected that where otters returned, kelp forests would <span class=blank></span>") + "Which choice most logically completes the text?", ["recover.", "disappear.", "stay the same.", "be eaten by otters."], 0, "Fewer urchins → more kelp.", "n"),
 q(P("Many assume that the first tool users were humans. However, stone tools found in Kenya are about 3.3 million years old, older than the earliest known members of our genus, <i>Homo</i>.") + "What does the text most strongly suggest?", ["Some species before Homo may have made stone tools.", "Humans lived 3.3 million years ago.", "The tools were made recently.", "Kenya has no fossils."], 0, "Tools predate Homo, so someone else made them.", "n"),
 q(P("The committee's response to the proposal was <span class=blank></span>; members neither approved nor rejected it, saying only that it needed \"further discussion.\"") + "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word?", ["noncommittal", "enthusiastic", "furious", "decisive"], 0, "Didn't commit either way.", "v"),
 q(P("Plant | Water per week (L) | Survived 6 weeks?", "Cactus | 0.2 | Yes", "Fern | 0.2 | No", "Fern | 1.5 | Yes") + "Which choice best supports the claim that ferns need more water than cacti?", ["With 0.2 L per week the cactus survived but the fern didn't; the fern survived with 1.5 L.", "The fern survived with 1.5 L.", "The cactus needed 0.2 L.", "Two ferns were tested."], 0, "Directly compares the two at the same water level.", "e"),
 q(P("Early photographers needed subjects to sit still for minutes. <u>As a result, many early portraits show stiff, unsmiling faces.</u>") + "Function of the underlined sentence?", ["It explains a consequence of the previous detail.", "It contradicts the previous sentence.", "It introduces a new topic.", "It gives the author's opinion of photography."], 0, "\"As a result\" = effect.", "s"),
 q(P("<b>Text 1:</b> Homework helps students learn because it gives them extra practice.", "<b>Text 2:</b> Homework helps older students, but studies of elementary school students find little benefit.") + "How would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to Text 1?", ["Its claim may hold for older students but not all students.", "Homework never helps anyone.", "Practice is useless.", "Elementary students need more homework."], 0, "Text 2 limits Text 1's claim.", "x"),
 q(P("The mathematician Katherine Johnson calculated flight paths for NASA by hand. In 1962, before John Glenn's orbit, he asked that she personally check the computer's numbers.") + "What does the text suggest about Glenn's view of Johnson?", ["He trusted her calculations.", "He doubted her work.", "He wanted to replace her with a computer.", "He didn't know who she was."], 0, "He asked for her check.", "n"),
 q(P("The town's new rules were meant to <span class=blank></span> noise at night, setting a 10 p.m. limit on loud music.") + "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word?", ["curb", "amplify", "praise", "measure"], 0, "Limit = curb.", "v"),
 q(P("Scientists once believed that adult brains couldn't form new neurons. Studies in the 1990s found new neurons forming in an adult brain region linked to memory, and research on how often this happens continues.") + "Best description of the structure?", ["An old belief, evidence against it, then ongoing research", "A theory confirmed by every study", "A list of brain regions", "A personal story"], 0, "Three moves in that order.", "s"),
 q(P("The author praises the city's parks but notes that most are in wealthy neighborhoods, leaving many residents a long walk from green space.") + "Which choice best describes the author's view?", ["Appreciative but critical of how parks are distributed", "Entirely negative", "Uninterested", "Opposed to parks"], 0, "Praise + concern.", "i"),
 q(P("A biologist claims that the frog's bright colors warn predators that it is toxic.") + "Which finding would most directly support the claim?", ["Birds that ate one bright frog avoided similar frogs afterward.", "The frogs live near rivers.", "The frogs are active at night.", "Bright frogs lay many eggs."], 0, "Shows predators learn from the colors.", "e"),
 q(P("The scientist's reputation was <span class=blank></span>; decades of careful, repeated studies made her one of the most trusted voices in the field.") + "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word?", ["unimpeachable", "questionable", "fleeting", "notorious"], 0, "Beyond doubt.", "v"),
 q(P("Some critics call graphic novels \"just comics.\" Yet several have won major literary prizes, and many schools now teach them alongside traditional novels.") + "What is the main purpose of the text?", ["To counter a dismissive view of graphic novels", "To explain how to draw comics", "To argue schools should ban novels", "To list literary prizes"], 0, "\"Yet\" pushes back on critics.", "s"),
]
build(C, "sat-2", L, 2, "Reading: Ideas, Evidence and Craft", 'Reading <span class="hl">like a test-maker</span>', "Main ideas, evidence from text and data, inferences, words in context, purpose, structure and paired texts.", "SAT Reading and Writing: Information and Ideas and Craft and Structure, with examples and practice passages.", CH, PQ, CHAL, EX, TAGS, OUT, minutes=18, exam_name="Reading set")
